## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — two new modules under `boukensha/`

**`boukensha/errors.py`** ← `lib/boukensha/errors.rb`. One line of substance:

```python
class UnknownToolError(Exception):
    """Raised when dispatch is called with a name that has no registered tool."""
```

Ruby subclasses `StandardError`; the Python analogue is `Exception` (**not**
`BaseException`, which would dodge normal `except` handling).

**`boukensha/registry.py`** ← `lib/boukensha/registry.rb`. A plain stateful
class holding a reference to the `Context`:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def initialize(context)` | `def __init__(self, context): self.context = context` |
| `def tool(name, description:, parameters: {}, &block)` | `def tool(self, name, *, description, parameters=None)` — keyword-only `description`/`parameters`, mirroring Ruby's required/optional keywords |
| `parameters: {}` default | `parameters=None` then `parameters or {}` — **never a mutable `{}` default** |
| `Tool.new(name.to_s, description, parameters, block)` | `Tool(str(name), description, parameters, block)` |
| `@context.register_tool(tool)` then `tool` | same — registers on the context and returns the `Tool` |
| `def dispatch(name, args = {})` | `def dispatch(self, name, args=None)` — same mutable-default rule |
| `@context.tools[name.to_s]` | `self.context.tools.get(str(name))` |
| `raise UnknownToolError, "No tool registered as '#{name}'" unless tool` | `raise UnknownToolError(f"No tool registered as '{name}'")` if falsy — **message string must match byte-for-byte**, single quotes around the name included |
| `tool.block.call(**args.transform_keys(&:to_sym))` | `tool.block(**args)` |

> **The symbol-key gotcha does not exist in Python.** Ruby needs
> `transform_keys(&:to_sym)` because the API hands back string-keyed JSON while
> Ruby blocks take symbol keywords. Python keyword arguments *are* strings, so
> `tool.block(**args)` works directly on the API's dict. Do not invent a
> translation step to "match" Ruby — the Ruby README calls this out as a
> learning point, and the honest port is that the problem vanishes. Say so in
> the Python README (see below).

